verdicts.port_notes:
Take 81ab48f7d unchanged onto tuned4:src/common/host_memory.cpp:1285 (IsZeroPage stops at the first non-zero 64-byte line).

In tuned4:src/core/device_memory.cpp TrimZeroPages:
- raise the backoff cap from 60 s to 300 s; keep the 5 s reset after a pass that trims at least 64 MiB, and keep the EDEN_NX2_SEC_RESERVE commit block (lines 54-67) where it is;
- take cd1582e0d's log line for every pass over 100 ms, so all passes show up in the log, not only the large trims;
- take 0f0e8470c's ZeroTrimPhases (host_memory.h/.cpp: query_us, scan_us, unlock_us, unlock_runs, longest), so the owner's log shows which phase is left after the early exit.

Take 68b9815db's EDEN_NX2_ZERO_TRIM=0 for one A/B launcher; it is an experiment switch, so it does not go upstream.

The 6a29460f5 bulk-mapping wait is optional. If you take it, add a plain std::atomic<u64> byte counter to HostMemory Map/Unmap/Protect (not the Nx2Hitch counters), and drop it unless the phase log shows guest map/protect calls stalling while a pass runs.

To verify on r50, in the same Ocarina route as r49, check that:
- the 'Zero-page pass ... page reads N ms' lines show page reads falling from about 1 s to tens of ms;
- the next pass is 300 s after a quiet one;
- the 1 s windows that contain a pass no longer show 5-18 frames over 30 ms (compare with the 74, 85, 107, 113, 125 and 146 s windows above);
- the 10 s '1% low' lines improve, against an EDEN_NX2_ZERO_TRIM=0 run as the ceiling.

The early exit is also worth offering upstream, since #961 is upstream code.

verdicts.reason:
The analyst's code findings hold:
- tuned4:src/video_core/fence_manager.h:121-124 does reserve(64) at every fence it makes;
- query_stream.h:149 uses std::deque<size_t> old_queries;
- the report std::function in query_cache.h allocates on the heap.

The benefit is small, though, and not measured. In r49 the back thread spends 37.8 ms/s in 'fence' (14,477/s), of which 23.3 ms/s is 'fence skipped' (9,440/s), where no list is made. About 4,500-5,300 fences a second are made (fence_manager.h:439 lines at 85-105 s). One 4 KiB reserve each, plus a deque block every two query-id pushes and one allocation per report, adds up to a few ms/s of back-thread time against 887 ms/s busy. That is about 1% at best, not a large benefit. Fritchii never measured these on their own: its v5.6 '+5 fps' credits them together with EXP61, which explains about half of it on its own, on an RTX 4080 with a different flush setup. The code is upstream Lichii's: 792e12a47 in open PR #1161 (upstream-pr/1161), not merged in 17baed716. It is bundled with fast-draw changes that our merge plan rated skip. Policy says to prefer upstream's version, or wait for it, when the same thing is in an open upstream PR. A hand cherry-pick of only the report/fence bullets also needs ReportOp adapted to tuned4's CounterReport payload, delayed_release, immediate path and FakeOcclusion, which is a moderate merge risk for a ~1% gain.
